---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const shape = `{
  "name": "Client number is required",
  "description": "Every client must carry an account number.",
  "expression": "ISBLANK(client_number)",
  "errorMessage": "Client Number cannot be blank",
  "isActive": true
}`;

const saveRules = `PUT /api/v1/objects/client
{
  "validationRules": [
    {
      "name": "Client number is required",
      "expression": "ISBLANK(client_number)",
      "errorMessage": "Client Number cannot be blank",
      "isActive": true
    }
  ]
}`;

const failure = `{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Client Number cannot be blank",
  "errors": ["Client Number cannot be blank"]
}`;

const EXAMPLES = [
  ['ISBLANK(client_number)', 'Client Number is required'],
  ['amount <= 0', 'Amount must be positive'],
  ['amount <= discount', 'Amount must exceed the discount'],
  ['ISBLANK(phone) AND ISBLANK(email)', 'At least one contact method is required'],
  ['start_date >= end_date', 'Start date must be before the end date'],
  ['close_date <= TODAY()', 'Close date must be in the future'],
  ['NOT CONTAINS(email, "@")', 'Email address must be valid'],
  ['status == "Active" AND probability < 50', 'Active deals need a probability of 50 or more'],
  ['subtotal > credit_limit', 'Order exceeds the client credit limit'],
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="Validation Rules"
  description="Cross-field rules that block a save, how they are evaluated, and the direction of the expression."
>
  <h1>Validation Rules</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A validation rule is a cross-field check evaluated every time a record is
    saved. Rules live on the object, alongside its fields.
  </p>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>The expression describes what to reject.</strong> A rule
    <em>triggers</em> — blocking the save — when its expression evaluates to
    <strong>true</strong>. So <code>ISBLANK(client_number)</code> means "reject a
    blank client number", not "require it to be blank". Writing the condition you
    want to <em>permit</em> is the single easiest mistake to make here, and it fails
    silently: the rule simply never fires.
  </div>

  <h2>Shape</h2>
  <pre><code>{shape}</code></pre>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Max 80 characters. Identifies the rule in errors when no message is set.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>No</td><td>Max 255 characters. For whoever maintains the rule.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>expression</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Max 1000 characters. Same syntax as <a href="/concepts/formula-fields">formula fields</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>errorMessage</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Max 255 characters. Returned verbatim — it is what the user reads.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>isActive</code></td><td>No</td><td>Defaults to <code>true</code>. Only an explicit <code>false</code> disables a rule.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Expression (blocks when true)</th><th>Intent</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {EXAMPLES.map(([expr, intent]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{expr}</code></td><td>{intent}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>When rules run</h2>
  <p>Rules are enforced on every write path:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>POST /records/:objectApiName</code> — create</li>
    <li><code>PUT</code> and <code>PATCH /records/:objectApiName/:id</code> — update</li>
    <li>CSV import — per row; failures are reported per row and don't abort the import</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On a partial (<code>PATCH</code>) update the rules run against the
    <strong>merged</strong> record, not just the submitted fields — so a partial
    update can't move a record into a state a rule forbids.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Rules can reference formula fields.</strong> Formula values are computed
    into the evaluation context before rules run, and recomputed from the incoming
    data on update — so <code>subtotal &gt; credit_limit</code> is checked against
    the subtotal this save will produce, not the one already stored.
  </div>

  <h2>Failure response</h2>
  <p>
    A triggered rule returns <code>400</code> with its message. All triggered rules
    and all field errors are collected first, so the response lists everything
    wrong at once:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{failure}</code></pre>

  <h2>Active and inactive</h2>
  <p>
    An inactive rule is kept but skipped on save. <code>isActive</code> defaults to
    <code>true</code>, and only an explicit <code>false</code> disables — a rule
    saved without the flag is still enforced.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only <strong>active</strong> rules must parse when you save the object. You can
    therefore park a half-finished rule as inactive; it is validated when you
    activate it.
  </p>

  <h2>Managing rules over the API</h2>
  <p>
    Rules are a property of the object. Sending <code>validationRules</code>
    <strong>replaces the entire array</strong> — read, modify, write back, or you
    will drop the rules you didn't send:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{saveRules}</code></pre>
  <p>
    An object may carry a bounded number of rules, and every active rule's
    expression must parse or the whole update is rejected with the offending rule
    named.
  </p>

  <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Direction.</strong> Say what to reject. If a rule never seems to fire,
      try inverting it — that is nearly always the cause.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Blank values.</strong> A missing field is <code>null</code>, and
      <code>null</code> coerces to <code>0</code> in comparisons. A rule like
      <code>amount &lt;= 0</code> will therefore reject a record where amount was
      never supplied. Guard with <code>NOT ISBLANK(amount) AND amount &lt;= 0</code>
      if that isn't what you want.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Required fields.</strong> For "this one field must be filled in", the
      field's own <code>isRequired</code> flag is the better tool — it marks the
      input as required in the UI and reports against that specific field. Reserve
      rules for conditions that span fields.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Broken expressions.</strong> A rule that fails to parse or throws is
      reported as a validation error naming the rule, not a <code>500</code>. The
      save is still blocked.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Previewing without saving</h2>
  <p>
    <code>POST /records/validate</code> runs field validation and the object's rules
    against a payload and returns what would fail — useful for validating a form
    before submit.
  </p>
</MainLayout>
